AB  - The rapid growth of mobile networks and the rising demand for uninterrupted connectivity have made telecom tower site selection a critical challenge for operators. This decision is complex, as it involves multiple conflicting factors such as coverage quality, population density, cost-effectiveness, regulatory compliance, and long-term scalability. Existing multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) methods, often struggle to effectively handle the uncertainty and imprecision inherent in expert judgments. Moreover, traditional models lack the ability to capture linguistic evaluations and nonlinear relationships in complex decision environments, creating a research gap in robust and flexible decision-support frameworks. To address this gap, we propose a novel hybrid decision-making model based on the improved evaluation based on distance from average solution (EDAS) method and the linguistic pq-rung orthopair neural network (Lpq-RONN) with the linguistic Aczel-Alsina aggregation operator using linguistic pq-rung orthopair fuzzy information (Lpq-ROFI). Initially, the fundamental principles of linguistic term sets, Aczel-Alsina norms, and the linguistic Aczel-Alsina aggregation operator are explored within the linguistic pq-rung orthopair fuzzy framework. To validate the proposed mode, we apply it to a real-world problem of selecting the most suitable location for a telecom tower. For this study, we collected the opinions of three experts on various site characteristics and applied the steps of the proposed model to reach the final decision. The results indicate that the Downtown Commercial Hub is the most suitable location, as it fulfills key requirements such as high network demand, coverage efficiency, and regulatory compliance. A sensitivity analysis was also conducted to evaluate how the model responds to changes in input parameters and to confirm the robustness of the decision-making process. Furthermore, we compared the performance of the proposed model with other existing decision-making methods. The findings demonstrate that our model is not only reliable and effective but also offers enhanced decision support for telecom tower site selection. Finally, the advantages and limitations of the proposed approach are discussed in the context of practical deployment scenarios.
