AB  - Broad learning systems (BLS) are efficient and effective classifiers that have been widely used in various fields recently. However, the presence of uncertain and redundant neuronal nodes hinders its satisfactory performance in complex classification tasks. Existing node selection methods struggle to automatically explore the optimal node selection strategy. Therefore, this paper employs a single-agent deep reinforcement learning method for node selection and proposes a novel reinforced node selection-based broad learning system (RNS-BLS). Firstly, a group relevance-based scanning strategy is proposed to initialize the node sequences of the BLS, thereby improving the optimization direction of the policy network. Then, a sparse coding-based state representation is proposed to encode node subsets and the agent’s location, thereby improving the accuracy and efficiency of environment modeling. Finally, a sparse Takagi–Sugeno fuzzy system is proposed to refine the action-value function further, thereby enhancing the stability of the node selection process. The effectiveness and stability of the proposed model are evaluated on several challenging machine learning public datasets and a self-constructed aluminum alloy x-ray fluorescence spectroscopy dataset. The results demonstrate that the proposed model exhibits high dynamic adaptability and superior predictive performance in classification tasks of varying complexity, outperforming numerous advanced models.
