AB  - Accurate three-dimensional reconstruction of transparent objects is a critical requirement for industrial digitalization, as reliable virtual three-dimensional models are essential for cyber-physical system development, production monitoring, and intelligent robotics. However, reconstructing transparent objects remains a fundamental challenge, as their complex refraction and transmission behaviors violate the assumptions of conventional three-dimensional reconstruction pipelines, including Gaussian splatting. To address this issue, we introduce a geometry-aware Gaussian splatting framework specifically developed for transparent-object reconstruction. The proposed method incorporates three key innovations: (1) a Transformer-based mask extraction module that produces transparency-aware boundary features to guide geometry localization; (2) a signed distance function-guided Gaussian refinement mechanism that enforces surface consistency and corrects depth ambiguity through geometry-aware supervision; and (3) a stereo-aligned novel-view synthesis strategy that generates virtual stereo pairs and fuses multi-view depth for robust cross-view geometric consistency. From the perspective of engineering application, we construct a benchmark dataset consisting of four synthetic and two real-world scenes of transparent objects, enabling systematic evaluation of reconstruction performance. Experimental results show that the proposed framework achieves improved reconstruction accuracy over representative baseline methods in both synthetic and real-world scenes, producing high-quality three-dimensional models that demonstrate potential for visualization and related industrial applications.
