AB  - Traditional methods for assessing the seismic responses of offshore wind turbines (OWTs) under multi-hazard conditions often overlook complex load interactions or require computationally prohibitive simulations, leading to potential underestimation of structural demands. This study introduces a novel automated deep model-fusion (ADMF) framework as a fast and accurate surrogate model to overcome these limitations. ADMF integrates multiple deep neural network (DNN) models, whose architectures are automatically optimized using the tree-structured Parzen estimator (TPE), and combines their predictions through advanced fusion techniques, including Bates-Granger Averaging (BGA), Granger-Ramanathan averaging (GRA), and Bayesian model averaging (BMA), to enhance predictive accuracy and generalization. The framework's efficacy is demonstrated on 5-megawatt (MW) and 10 MW OWT models using a comprehensive dataset derived from 48,000 nonlinear dynamic analyses, covering a wide range of structural, geotechnical, and environmental uncertainties. Compared to the top performer among 16 tuned benchmark models, the BMA-based ADMF achieved significantly lower mean absolute error (MAE), reducing it by 18.75 %–32.4 % across the analyzed scenarios. A comprehensive Sobol global sensitivity analysis further enhances the study by identifying and ranking the most influential input parameters, such as environmental loads and material properties that govern the OWT's dynamic behavior. Overall, the ADMF framework demonstrates superior performance and robustness, presenting a powerful tool for rapid, risk-informed assessment of OWTs under complex environmental loading.
