AB  - The development of efficient and precise long-term traffic prediction technologies is crucial for urban travel planning and traffic management decision-making. However, current models often encounter significant increases in Time complexity or Space complexity when attempting to enhance the performance of long-term traffic predictions, which poses substantial challenges for their practical deployment in industrial contexts. To mitigate this issue, this study introduces an Inner-Outer Contrastive Representation Learning Model for Traffic Prediction (IO-CRLT). To model the seasonal feature, a time position embedding module is implemented to encode time-series features at multiple granularities, thereby improving the model's capacity to discern seasonal patterns within traffic data. Furthermore, a season-trend disentangling mechanism is established through contrastive learning of weak and strong augment sequences, in conjunction with an adaptive noise filter, to significantly mitigate the complexity associated with modeling potential seasonal characteristics of trends. Building on this foundation, an inner sequence contrastive learning mechanism is developed by analyzing the autocorrelation of various slide windows within a mini-batch. This approach facilitates the modeling of global correlations that extend beyond the limitation of the window size. Moreover, the complexity of model training is alleviated through the implementation of grouping and masking strategies. In order to better illustrate the progressiveness of IO-CRLT, we have applied it to the classic data set of traffic prediction tasks, experimental results demonstrate that the proposed IO-CRLT model surpasses the performance of the most advanced methodologies across three traffic datasets, and achieved excellent results on the 36 step long-term prediction task of METR-LA dataset.
