AB  - Wearable robotic exoskeleton (WRE) design evaluation is critical during development, as it directly affects subsequent development costs and customer satisfaction. However, current research often neglects three key aspects: the validity of customer evaluations, heterogeneous trust relationships among experts, and evaluation hesitation. To bridge these gaps, this study introduces a novel two-sided driven framework that employs multi-granularity trapezoidal interval type-2 fuzzy sets (TrIT2FSs) to model hesitation and uncertainty. On the customer side, the validity of customer requirements (CRs) is ensured by filtering inputs against expert judgment. On the expert side, social network analysis (SNA) quantifies trust relationships to derive expert weights. A hybrid procedure integrating the Criteria Importance Through Intercriteria Correlation (CRITIC) method and the Full Consistency Method (FUCOM) under the TrIT2FS environment (Tr-CRITIC–FUCOM) computes the weights of design requirements (DRs). Subsequently, an integrated Interactive Multi-Criteria Decision-Making (TODIM) and Vlsekriterijumska Optimizacija I Kompromisno Rešenje (VIKOR) method (Tr-TODIM–VIKOR) is developed to generate adaptive rankings for identifying the optimal design. A case study demonstrates that the framework yields more reliable decisions and more stable rankings than benchmark methods. Further comparative validation and sensitivity analysis confirm the approach’s robustness and practical applicability. This framework provides a systematic tool for integrating multi-source fuzzy information into early-stage design decisions.
