AB  - In classification tasks, especially when the goal is to accurately identify minority classes, the data imbalance problem emerges as a significant hurdle. This presents a substantial challenge, notably when the classes we aim to predict are underrepresented. Real-world applications frequently present multi-class imbalance problems, which are more complex than the binary case. Error-correcting output codes offer a handy technique to transform multi-class problems into multiple binary classification problems. This study proposes a framework to address the multi-class imbalance problem through two approaches: (1) extending existing sampling methods for binary classification and ensemble learning techniques; (2) integrating the approach with the error-correcting output codes. With proper code matrices, our method specifically targets mitigating the class imbalance issue within the resulting binary sub-problems (dichotomies) generated by the code matrix. The experiments confirm the effectiveness of these methods in handling imbalanced data, particularly when measured using the multi-class area under the curve metric and geometric-mean metric.
