AB  - The integration of drone technology with visual detection techniques has opened up new opportunities in the field of deep learning-based object detection. However, these opportunities are often accompanied by significant challenges. Images captured from drone perspectives typically exhibit characteristics such as small target sizes, complex backgrounds, and frequent occlusions, which collectively hinder detection performance. Moreover, most existing approaches are based on incremental improvements to established algorithmic strategies. However, these improvements often overlook the inherent limitations of the original algorithms in detecting small objects, leading to only marginal gains in accuracy while increasing the complexity of network structures. To address these challenges, we propose a dynamic pruning-based small object detection algorithm for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), named Dynamic Pruning for UAV Detection-You Only Look Once (DPUD-YOLO). First, we introduce a feature fusion module called Adaptive Enhanced Spatial Pyramid Pooling (AESPPF), which enhances attention to small object features by integrating the Shuffle Attention mechanism. It further incorporates global average pooling and max pooling to enrich contextual information and extract multi-scale features. In addition, we modify the neck's output structure by removing low-resolution outputs that are unsuitable for small object detection and introducing high-resolution outputs. Based on this, we upgrade the coupled detection head to an efficient and lightweight decoupled detection head (EL-Decoupled Head), which separates regression and classification tasks to improve detection accuracy. In particular, we design a feature extraction module named Dynamic Pruning Bottleneck (DPB), which optimizes feature processing by pruning redundant information while retaining essential shallow features, thereby achieving efficient feature extraction and organization. Experimental results on the VisDrone2019 dataset demonstrate that our algorithm achieves a mean average precision (mAP@0.5) of 41.8 %, outperforming the baseline, You Only Look Once version 8 (YOLOv8), You Only Look Once version 9 (YOLOv9), and You Only Look Once version 10 (YOLOv10) by 8.7 %, 2.6 %, 0.5 %, and 2.2 %, respectively. These results indicate the superior detection performance of our method. Furthermore, improvements in other evaluation metrics are consistently supported by evidence from both ablation and comparative experiments.
