KW  - Graph data
KW  - Probabilistic
KW  - Prediction
KW  - Civil engineering application
KW  - Neural networks
AB  - This study aims to develop a universal framework, termed GraphMixS, for predicting dynamic responses of structures subjected to time-varying ground motions. The originality of the proposed framework lies in its generalization capability: a single model can address a wide range of structural configurations. This distinguishes it from most existing deep learning-based models which are typically limited to specific structural layouts. To achieve this versatility, first, the geometric configurations of structures, including dimension, cross-sections, element connectivity, and material properties are transformed into equivalent graph data. Next, a data aggregation block aggregates different input types, involving graph data, time-varying data, and historical outputs, into unified multidimensional tensors. After that, a data representation learning block based on the mixed-strategy deep learning architecture is employed to extract information relevant to the prediction task. Lastly, a multiple-layer perceptron prediction head is designed to provide multi-channel, multi-step, and multi-quantile outputs. In addition, a customized distribution function loss is proposed, enabling the GraphMixS framework to not only provide predictions but also corresponding uncertainty quantification. The applicability and performance of GraphMixS are consistently demonstrated via 20 structural problems encompassing both two-dimensional and complex three-dimensional multi-story building structures.
